.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The function uses a helper \f[CR]v2_wfd()\f[R] to determine the write
file descriptor, which may differ from the read fd in pipe\-based
transports (where \f[CR]session\->write_fd\f[R] is set separately).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]CHAN_CONTROL\f[R] handler processes \f[CR]auth\f[R],
\f[CR]ping\f[R], \f[CR]disconnect\f[R], and \f[CR]configure\f[R] message
types.
On successful auth, it calls \f[CR]init_bash_for_session()\f[R],
\f[CR]observe_init()\f[R], and \f[CR]debug_init()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]CHAN_COMMAND\f[R] handler captures stdout/stderr via temp
files and dup2, sends base64\-encoded output as
\f[CR]stdout\f[R]/\f[CR]stderr\f[R] frames, and a \f[CR]complete\f[R]
frame with the exit code.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]CHAN_STATE\f[R] handler bridges v2 JSON messages to v1 state
handlers by using a pipe to capture v1 text responses and converting
them to JSON.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]CHAN_PTY\f[R] handler (\f[CR]pty_handle_spawn\f[R]) takes over
the session loop for the duration of the PTY session, then returns
control to the main frame loop.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]CHAN_OBSERVE\f[R] handler manages event subscription levels
via \f[CR]observe_set_level()\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Each payload is freed after dispatch.
Channel handlers must not store or return references to the payload
buffer.
.IP \(bu 2
Cleanup functions (\f[CR]debug_cleanup\f[R], \f[CR]observe_cleanup\f[R])
are always called on exit, regardless of the reason for session
termination.
